    },
    {
      "line": 33,
      "text": "`ORIGIN_IS_NOT_A_CELL`."
    },
    {
      "line": 38,
      "text": "2. `CIRCLE_CELL` — the closed circular carrier around that center;"
    },
    {
      "line": 43,
      "text": "Cell centers form the same gap-free triangular carrier arrangement. Normalize nearest-neighbor center spacing to"
    },
    {
      "line": 45,
      "text": "`D_CENTER = 1`."
    },
    {
      "line": 49,
      "text": "`NEAREST_CELL_CENTER_DISTANCE = 1`."
    },
    {
      "line": 51,
      "text": "Let the three positive native axis rays be `E_1,E_2,E_3`. In the planar carrier presentation their directions occur successively at `120 degree` separation."
    },
    {
      "line": 55,
      "text": "`e_1+e_2+e_3=0`"
    },
    {
      "line": 57,
      "text": "was a relation of a classical planar vector presentation of the three carrier directions. It is **not** a native Enterprise vector identity and must not be used to define native length, opposite directions, or primitive native-point coordinate equivalence."
    },
    {
      "line": 67,
      "text": "No native negative axes are required. The three positive rays themselves partition the plane into three native sectors."
    },
    {
      "line": 71,
      "text": "`ENTERPRISE_NATIVE_AXES = THREE_POSITIVE_RAYS`."
    },
    {
      "line": 73,
      "text": "`NO_NATIVE_NEGATIVE_AXES_REQUIRED`."
    },
    {
      "line": 111,
      "text": "Cell centers carry integer addresses in this sector atlas. Because the geometric origin is a triple-intersection vertex rather than a center, the center lattice is an affine carrier relative to the origin; integer address does not mean that a center lies on a number axis."
    },
    {
      "line": 117,
      "text": "`R_CELL = 1/sqrt(3)`."
    },
    {
      "line": 123,
      "text": "With nearest center spacing 1, this is the critical radius at which the three cells centered at the vertices of every elementary unit center triangle meet at one common boundary point."
    },
    {
      "line": 129,
      "text": "`D_CENTER = 1 < 2/sqrt(3)`."
    },
    {
      "line": 131,
      "text": "Therefore neighboring circle cells overlap with positive area."
    },
    {
      "line": 137,
      "text": "At `R_CELL=1/sqrt(3)`, every elementary three-center triangle has one common triple boundary-intersection point. The next nonzero center separation is too large for non-neighbor circles to intersect. Hence every circle-boundary crossing in the carrier occurs as a three-cell intersection rather than as an isolated two-cell crossing."
    },
    {
      "line": 141,
      "text": "`EVERY_CELL_BOUNDARY_INTERSECTION_IS_TRIPLE`."
    },
    {
      "line": 145,
      "text": "At nearest center spacing 1, radius `1/sqrt(3)` is exactly the covering threshold of this triangular center carrier. Therefore"
    },
    {
      "line": 147,
      "text": "`UNION_ALL_ENTERPRISE_CELLS = ENTERPRISE_PLANE`."
    },
    {
      "line": 153,
      "text": "`DENSE_DISCRETE = DISCRETE_INTEGER_CENTER_ADDRESSES + GAP_FREE_OVERLAPPING_CIRCLE_CELLS`."
    },
    {
      "line": 155,
      "text": "At the critical radius, three neighboring cells have a common set consisting of a single triple-intersection point rather than a positive-area three-cell overlap region."
    },
    {
      "line": 261,
      "text": "Triple-intersection vertices are transition/incidence events between cells, not simultaneous multi-cell states."
    }
